POSTS={
"why-pipes-burst-in-florida":{
"title":"Why Pipes Burst in Florida Homes With No Freeze",
"desc":"Pipes burst in Largo without a freeze. Age, water chemistry and failed supply hoses cause most of it. Here is what actually fails.",
"h1":"Why Pipes Burst in Florida Homes With No Freeze",
"eyebrow":"Sources of water damage","img":"water-damage-hallway-air-movers-largo-home.jpg",
"intro":[
 "Pipes burst in Florida homes all year, and almost none of it has anything to do with cold. In Largo the causes are age, water chemistry, pressure and a handful of components that were never built to last as long as owners assume.",
 "Knowing which part of your plumbing is most likely to fail tells you where to look and what to replace before it floods a room. Most of the failures we see come from a short list."],
"sections":[
 ("Supply Hoses Fail More Than Pipes Do",[
  "The flexible hose feeding a toilet, sink or washing machine is the single most common failure we attend. Rubber washing machine hoses harden and split. Braided stainless lines corrode at the crimp where the braid meets the fitting.",
  "These parts cost very little and are rated for years, not decades. Braided lines are typically replaced every five to seven years, and rubber hoses sooner.",
  "A failed hose behind a washing machine runs at full pressure until someone finds it. That is why an overnight or holiday failure so often reaches several rooms."]),
 ("Polybutylene and Early CPVC",[
  "Homes built in Largo and the surrounding Pinellas communities during the 1980s and early 1990s often carry grey polybutylene supply pipe with plastic fittings. The material becomes brittle with age and chlorinated water, and failures tend to happen at the fittings.",
  "Early CPVC has a similar story. It performs well for years, then becomes brittle enough that a fitting cracks under normal pressure.",
  "Check under a sink. Grey flexible pipe with plastic fittings usually means polybutylene, and it is worth a conversation with a licensed plumber about a repipe."]),
 ("Corroded Galvanized Supply Lines",[
  "Older Largo homes that were never repiped may still have galvanized steel supply. It corrodes from the inside, which narrows the bore and weakens the wall at the same time.",
  "Warning signs are discoloured water on first draw, dropping pressure at fixtures furthest from the meter, and staining around joints."]),
 ("Water Heaters on a Bare Slab",[
  "A tank sitting directly on a garage or closet slab with no pan is a common arrangement here and a costly one. When the tank lets go it releases forty gallons or more at once, and on a slab that water spreads sideways into the hallway and adjoining rooms.",
  "A drain pan with a routed line turns that event into a wet garage floor. It is inexpensive work and it contains most of the damage."]),
 ("Pressure and Water Chemistry",[
  "High static pressure stresses every fitting in the house. Anything consistently above about eighty pounds per square inch shortens component life, and a pressure reducing valve that has failed open is easy to miss.",
  ("table",["Factor","Effect on plumbing"],
   [["Static pressure above 80 psi","Accelerates fitting and hose failure throughout the system"],
    ["Chlorinated supply","Embrittles polybutylene and early CPVC over time"],
    ["Thermal expansion","Stresses joints when there is no expansion tank on a closed system"],
    ["Age of flexible connectors","The most common single point of failure in a Florida home"]])]),
 ("What Fails by Decade of Construction",[
  ("table",["Build era","Most likely failure"],
   [["1950s to 1960s","Galvanized supply and cast iron drain lines under the slab"],
    ["1970s","Mixed copper and galvanized, aging fixture connectors"],
    ["1980s to early 1990s","Polybutylene fittings, early CPVC"],
    ["Late 1990s onward","Supply hoses, water heaters, appliance connections"]])]),
 ("What to Check This Month",[
  ("ul",["Replace rubber washing machine hoses with braided stainless",
         "Close laundry valves when you travel",
         "Look under sinks for grey polybutylene pipe",
         "Confirm the water heater pan drains outside",
         "Test static pressure and the pressure reducing valve",
         "Note where your main shutoff is before you need it"])])],
"guides_h2":"Related Guides on What Causes Water Damage",
"linkblurbs":[
 "What polybutylene looks like, which homes have it and what a repipe involves.",
 "Why repeat backups in an older Largo home point at the drain line, not the paper.",
 "Why a tank on a bare garage slab turns into a hallway loss.",
 "The order to work in when a supply line lets go inside a wall."],
"svcblurbs":[
 "Extraction, wall cavity drying and repair after a failed supply line or valve.",
 "Drying after washing machine, dishwasher, refrigerator and water heater failures."],
"faq_h2":"Burst Pipe Questions From Florida Homeowners",
"faqs":[
 ("Can pipes burst in Florida without freezing weather?","Yes, and most do. Age, water chemistry, pressure and failed flexible connectors cause the great majority of burst pipe losses in Pinellas County."),
 ("What is the most common plumbing failure in a Largo home?","A flexible supply hose at a toilet, sink or washing machine. They are inexpensive parts with a service life measured in years rather than decades."),
 ("How often should supply lines be replaced?","Braided stainless lines are typically replaced every five to seven years. Rubber washing machine hoses should be replaced sooner or swapped for braided."),
 ("How do I know if I have polybutylene pipe?","Look under a sink for grey flexible pipe with plastic fittings. Homes built in the 1980s and early 1990s that were never repiped often still have it."),
 ("Does polybutylene affect my insurance?","It can. Some Florida insurers ask about it at renewal and a few decline or surcharge. Ask your agent directly rather than waiting for a claim."),
 ("What water pressure is too high?","Anything consistently above about eighty pounds per square inch stresses fittings and shortens the life of hoses and valves throughout the house."),
 ("Should my water heater have a drain pan?","Yes, with a line routed to the outside. Without one, a tank failure releases forty gallons or more straight onto the slab and into adjoining rooms."),
 ("Can a burst pipe inside a wall be dried without opening it?","Often yes. Air is injected into the cavity through small holes and readings are taken daily. If the cavity will not reach a dry standard, a section comes out."),
 ("Is a burst pipe covered by homeowners insurance?","A sudden accidental discharge is generally covered. Long term seepage from a leak that was ignored usually is not, which is why the timeline matters."),
 ("What should I do the moment I find a burst pipe?","Shut the water off at the main or the fixture stop, cut power to wet outlets if it is safe, then photograph everything before moving anything.")]},

"mold-vs-mildew-florida":{
"title":"Mold or Mildew in a Florida Home: How to Tell",
"desc":"Mildew wipes off a surface. Mold has roots in the material. Here is how Largo homeowners tell the difference and what each one needs.",
"h1":"Mold or Mildew in a Florida Home: How to Tell Them Apart",
"eyebrow":"Mold","img":"tile-floor-injection-drying-largo-home.jpg",
"intro":[
 "Mold and mildew get used as if they mean the same thing, and in a Florida home the difference decides whether you clean a surface or remove material. Mildew sits on top of a surface. Mold grows into it.",
 "Largo homes give both plenty of opportunity. High dew points, slab construction and air conditioned surfaces that stay cool enough to collect moisture make growth easy. Telling them apart takes about a minute."],
"sections":[
 ("The Practical Difference",[
  ("table",["","Mildew","Mold"],
   [["Where it sits","On the surface","Into the material"],
    ["Appearance","Flat, powdery, grey or white","Fuzzy or slimy, green, black, grey or brown"],
    ["Typical location","Tile grout, shower, window sill","Drywall, wood, insulation, carpet pad"],
    ["Response","Clean the surface","Find the moisture source and remove affected porous material"]]),
  "A simple test: wipe it. If the surface comes clean and stays clean, you were dealing with mildew and a humidity problem. If it reappears in the same place, or the material underneath is soft or stained, you have mold with a moisture source behind it."]),
 ("Why Florida Makes Both Common",[
  "Pinellas County dew points sit in the seventies for much of the year. Warm, wet air meeting an air conditioned wall or a cool tile surface produces condensation, and condensation is all mildew needs.",
  "Mold needs more. It needs a material it can feed on, which means drywall paper, wood, insulation or carpet pad, and it needs that material to stay wet."]),
 ("Where Mold Hides in a Largo House",[
  ("ul",["Behind baseboards where a slab leak wicked up the drywall",
         "Inside wall cavities after a supply line failure",
         "Under tile set directly on slab",
         "In the air handler closet around a rusted condensate pan",
         "Under carpet pad in a room that was wet and dried only on the surface",
         "In attic insulation under a roof leak"])]),
 ("When Cleaning Is Enough",[
  "On hard, non porous surfaces like tile, glass and sealed countertops, cleaning works. Detergent and a cloth remove surface growth, and keeping the area dry keeps it away.",
  "On drywall, bare wood, insulation and carpet pad it does not. Those materials are porous, growth reaches below the surface, and spraying them wets the material further without reaching the root."]),
 ("Why Bleach Is the Wrong Tool Indoors",[
  "Bleach works on hard non porous surfaces. On porous material it mostly adds water. The bleach stays near the surface, the water soaks in, and the growth often returns stronger.",
  "Removing the moisture source and removing affected porous material is what actually resolves it."]),
 ("What Both Problems Have in Common",[
  "Neither is solved by treating what you can see. Mildew returns if the humidity stays high. Mold returns if the leak is still there.",
  "Correct the moisture first, every time. In Largo that usually means fixing a leak, clearing a condensate line or getting indoor humidity down and holding it there."])],
"guides_h2":"Related Mold Guides for Pinellas County",
"linkblurbs":[
 "Where to look when the smell is clear but nothing is visible.",
 "The signs that growth is inside the wall rather than on it.",
 "What the common claims about black mold actually get wrong.",
 "How fast growth starts on wet material in Florida conditions."],
"svcblurbs":[
 "Containment, HEPA filtration, removal and verification for humid slab homes.",
 "Moisture mapping, thermal imaging and air sampling with a written report."],
"faq_h2":"Mold and Mildew Questions From Largo Homeowners",
"faqs":[
 ("What is the difference between mold and mildew?","Mildew sits on the surface and wipes off. Mold grows into the material and needs the moisture source corrected and affected porous material removed."),
 ("Is mildew dangerous?","Mildew is mainly a cleaning and humidity problem. It can irritate people with asthma or allergies, but it does not damage material the way mold does."),
 ("Can I clean mold myself?","On hard non porous surfaces like tile and glass, yes. On drywall, wood, insulation or carpet pad, cleaning does not reach the growth below the surface."),
 ("Does bleach kill mold?","On hard non porous surfaces it helps. On porous material it adds water without reaching the root growth, and the problem often returns worse."),
 ("Why does mildew keep coming back in my bathroom?","Because the humidity has not changed. Improve ventilation, run the exhaust fan longer and keep indoor relative humidity down, or it will return in the same spots."),
 ("What does mold smell like?","Earthy or musty, often described as a damp basement smell. In Florida homes it is frequently noticed before anything is visible."),
 ("How quickly does mold grow after water damage?","On wet porous material in Florida humidity, growth can begin within about twenty four to forty eight hours."),
 ("Do I need testing to confirm mold?","Not always. Visible growth with a known moisture source can be scoped directly. Testing helps when the source is unclear or a written protocol is needed."),
 ("Can mold grow without a leak?","Yes. Chronic high humidity, a condensate drip or condensation on cool surfaces will grow mold with no standing water ever appearing."),
 ("What humidity level prevents mold indoors?","Keeping indoor relative humidity below about sixty percent, and ideally nearer fifty, removes the conditions mold needs to establish.")]},
}
